# Type scale

Take every text size from this scale. Do not make up a new size for one card.

Each role has one size on wide screens and one on narrow screens, such as a phone. The Civica theme multiplies every size by Home Assistant's font size scale, `--ha-font-size-scale`, so all text grows together if the scale is raised. Browser and app zoom make it larger too.

## Sizes for each role

| Role | Wide screens | Narrow screens | Line height | Weight | Civica or Home Assistant variable |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Page heading | 48px | 36px | 1.1 | Bold | None. Used on documentation pages and custom panels only |
| Section heading | 30px | 24px | 1.2 | Bold | None. Used on documentation pages and custom panels only |
| Card heading | 24px | 20px to 24px | 1.25 | Bold | `civica-font-size-heading` |
| Main reading | 32px to 48px | Large enough to fit the value and unit | 1.25, from `ha-line-height-condensed` | Regular or bold | None. Size it to fit the card |
| Body text and main controls | 19px | 18px to 19px | 1.5 | Regular | `civica-font-size-body` |
| Supporting text | 16px | 16px | 1.5 | Regular | `civica-font-size-small` |

These sizes are for maintained Civica cards and panels. On a phone, the narrow sizes stop a heading from taking the whole screen.

## Specimen

This specimen sets each role at its wide-screen size, with text from an invented household's dashboard. On a phone, the page and section headings use their narrow sizes instead.

<%=
  samples = [
    ['Hughes home', 'Page heading', 48, 700, 1.1],
    ['Downstairs', 'Section heading', 30, 700, 1.2],
    ['Living room', 'Card heading', 24, 700, 1.25],
    ['21.4°C', 'Main reading, shown here at 40px', 40, 400, 1.25],
    ['The heating is on until 22:00.', 'Body text and main controls', 19, 400, 1.5],
    ['Updated 14:05', 'Supporting text', 16, 400, 1.5]
  ]
  items = samples.map do |text, role, size, weight, line|
    detail = role.include?('40px') ? "#{role}, #{weight == 700 ? 'bold' : 'regular'}, line height #{line}" : "#{role}, #{size}px #{weight == 700 ? 'bold' : 'regular'}, line height #{line}"
    %(<div style="margin:0 0 20px"><p style="font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:#{size}px;font-weight:#{weight};line-height:#{line};margin:0;overflow-wrap:anywhere">#{text}</p><p style="font-size:16px;line-height:1.5;margin:5px 0 0">#{detail}</p></div>)
  end
  %(<figure style="margin:0 0 30px">#{items.join}<figcaption>Each role at its wide-screen size. The large text is a sample, not a heading, so it is not marked up as one.</figcaption></figure>)
%>

## Wide and narrow screens compared

This comparison shows how much each size changes between wide and narrow screens. Headings change most. Body and supporting text barely change, so they stay easy to read on a phone.

<%=
  hex = ->(id) { govuk_colour(id).fetch('hex') }
  ink, grid, wide_c, narrow_c = hex.('black'), hex.('black-tint-80'), hex.('blue'), hex.('magenta')
  roles = [
    ['Page heading', [48, 48], [36, 36]],
    ['Section heading', [30, 30], [24, 24]],
    ['Card heading', [24, 24], [20, 24]],
    ['Main reading', [32, 48], nil],
    ['Body text and main controls', [19, 19], [18, 19]],
    ['Supporting text', [16, 16], [16, 16]]
  ]
  x_of = ->(px) { 60 + px * 4 }
  label = ->(r) { r[0] == r[1] ? "#{r[0]}px" : "#{r[0]} to #{r[1]}px" }
  bar = lambda do |range, y, colour|
    out = %(<rect x="60" y="#{y}" width="#{range[0] * 4}" height="12" fill="#{colour}"/>)
    out += %(<rect x="#{x_of.(range[0])}" y="#{y + 1}" width="#{(range[1] - range[0]) * 4}" height="10" fill="none" stroke="#{colour}" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="4 2"/>) if range[1] > range[0]
    out + %(<text x="#{x_of.(range[1]) + 5}" y="#{y + 10}" font-size="10" fill="#{ink}">#{label.(range)}</text>)
  end
  svg = []
  [0, 12, 24, 36, 48].each { |v| svg << %(<line x1="#{x_of.(v)}" y1="8" x2="#{x_of.(v)}" y2="272" stroke="#{grid}" stroke-width="1"/><text x="#{x_of.(v)}" y="286" font-size="10" text-anchor="middle" fill="#{ink}">#{v}</text>) }
  svg << %(<text x="#{x_of.(48)}" y="299" font-size="10" text-anchor="middle" fill="#{ink}">Size (px)</text>)
  roles.each_with_index do |(role, wide, narrow), i|
    y = 18 + i * 44
    svg << %(<text x="0" y="#{y}" font-size="11" font-weight="700" fill="#{ink}">#{role}</text>)
    svg << %(<text x="0" y="#{y + 14}" font-size="10" fill="#{ink}">Wide</text>) << bar.(wide, y + 4, wide_c)
    svg << %(<text x="0" y="#{y + 29}" font-size="10" fill="#{ink}">Narrow</text>)
    svg << (narrow ? bar.(narrow, y + 19, narrow_c) : %(<text x="62" y="#{y + 29}" font-size="10" fill="#{ink}">Not fixed: large enough to fit the value and unit</text>))
  end
  rows = roles.map { |role, wide, narrow| %(<tr class="govuk-table__row"><th scope="row" class="govuk-table__header">#{role}</th><td class="govuk-table__cell">#{label.(wide)}</td><td class="govuk-table__cell">#{narrow ? label.(narrow) : 'Not fixed. Large enough to fit the value and unit.'}</td></tr>) }
  %(<div class="c-colour-example"><figure style="margin:0 0 20px">) +
    %(<svg role="img" aria-labelledby="scale-compared-title scale-compared-desc" viewBox="0 0 320 304" style="display:block;width:100%;max-width:480px;height:auto;font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif"><title id="scale-compared-title">Bar chart comparing each type size on wide and narrow screens</title><desc id="scale-compared-desc">Each role has a wide-screen bar and a narrow-screen bar, labelled Wide and Narrow, with the size in pixels at the end of each bar. A solid bar shows a fixed size, and a dashed outline after it shows a range. The page heading drops from 48px to 36px and the section heading from 30px to 24px. The card heading is 24px wide and 20 to 24px narrow. The main reading is 32 to 48px wide and not fixed on narrow screens, so it has no narrow bar. Body text is 19px wide and 18 to 19px narrow. Supporting text is 16px on both. The table after the chart gives every size.</desc><g aria-hidden="true">#{svg.join}</g></svg>) +
    %(<figcaption>Type sizes in px on wide and narrow screens, before the font size scale. A dashed outline shows a range of sizes. A role with no narrow bar has no fixed narrow size: it is not 0.</figcaption></figure>) +
    %(<details class="govuk-details"><summary class="govuk-details__summary"><span class="govuk-details__summary-text">Show the data: type sizes on wide and narrow screens</span></summary><div class="govuk-details__text"><div class="civica-table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Type sizes on wide and narrow screens"><table class="govuk-table"><caption class="govuk-table__caption govuk-table__caption--s">Type sizes on wide and narrow screens</caption><thead class="govuk-table__head"><tr class="govuk-table__row"><th scope="col" class="govuk-table__header">Role</th><th scope="col" class="govuk-table__header">Wide screens</th><th scope="col" class="govuk-table__header">Narrow screens</th></tr></thead><tbody class="govuk-table__body">#{rows.join}</tbody></table></div></div></details></div>)
%>

<h2 id="native-home-assistant-sizes">Native Home Assistant sizes</h2>

Native Home Assistant screens are denser than a Civica card, so they keep smaller sizes. The Civica theme raises each native size by one step, to these values:

| Home Assistant variable | Size | Used for |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `ha-font-size-l` | 19px | Larger native text |
| `ha-font-size-m` | 16px | Native controls and body text |
| `ha-font-size-s` | 14px | Supporting labels |
| `ha-font-size-xs` | 12px | Compact extra text, such as a secondary detail in a row |

[Web-app typography](typography-web-app.html#bg-typ-04) explains why native screens stay dense.

## Set sizes in a card

Use the variable, not a pixel value, so the size follows the font size scale. Each variable is written as a `calc()` that multiplies the base size by `--ha-font-size-scale`.

```css
.reading-label {
  font-size: var(--civica-font-size-small);
  line-height: 1.5;
}
```


Heading cards already use the card heading size. [Headings](typography-headings.html#heading-cards) explains how.
